pe  For an initial review, make the checking visible across the complete change. Go through it declaration by declaration, or in coherent groups of related statements, and say plainly what you checked within your dimension.  For a follow-up review, use the supplied previous review, contributor message, and reverification diff. Recheck only affected prior findings and newly changed material. Do not repeat unchanged verification from the earlier review; one sentence may state that unaffected checks carry forward.  If the reverification diff does not affect your prior finding or your review dimension, publish only a concise exact-head decision. Keep the required headings, but under `Reverified` say that the previous review still holds and why the delta is irrelevant in one sentence. Do not restate the prior review, repeat declaration-by-declaration checks, or manufacture new detail.  For each point:  - Name the exact declaration and its location (file, line, head commit) so a   reader can go straight to it, and quote the relevant statement or proof line   when that makes the point concrete. - For a concrete code finding, prefer a line-specific review comment/thread on   the changed line when Gitea tooling supports it. If you cannot publish an   inline comment non-interactively, include the exact file, line, declaration,   and a short quoted code snippet in the `Findings` bullet so the discussion is   anchored to the code. - Separate what you VERIFIED (checked and found sound) from what you are FLAGGING   (a finding). Both belong in the review: the verified points show your coverage,   the findings show the problems. Write the positive checks explicitly — e.g.   "the statement carries the explicit `[ConnectedSpace M]` hypothesis" or "the   proof establishes exactly the stated claim, not a weaker variant" — not only   the objections. - For a finding, give the concrete fix and, separately, why it matters, and   guard the scope: say what should NOT change, so the contributor does not   overcorrect.  ## Relevant precedent  When a finding turns on a non-obvious mathematical interpretation, definition identity, coercion, canonical form, API shape, generality, import, attribute, or instance choice, consider looking for closely related current mathlib code, documentation, pull-request discussion, or public Lean Zulip discussion. Use it only when it makes the review more precise; routine checks do not need citations.  Current code and documentation outweigh older discussions. Link what you actually consulted, explain the relevant similarity and difference, and do not say a proposal was rejected unless the source records that outcome. An inline link is normally enough. Use a short code or prose excerpt only when its exact form matters, or add a compact `References consulted` section when several sources materially informed the decision. Optional Zulip unavailability is not a review blocker.  ## Required Markdown structure  Use this compact structure. Omit `Optional` when empty, but keep the other headings. Under `Findings`, write `None.` when there are no findings.  ```markdown ## Decision  Approved. <!-- or: Changes requested. / Comment only. -->  ## Findings  - **Blocking/Non-blocking — `file:line` / declaration.** Finding, consequence,   concrete fix, and scope guard.  ## Verified  - **`file:line` / declaration.** What was checked and why it is sound.  ## Optional  - Concise non-blocking improvement.  ## References consulted  - Link and one-line relevance, only when references materially informed the review. ```  For a follow-up, rename `Verified` to `Reverified` and include only the affected items. `Optional` and `References consulted` are both optional. Brevity is right when the delta is small.  ## Skill: mathlib-documentation  --- name: mathlib-documentation description: Locate and apply current mathlib and Lean review, naming, style, and documentation conventions. ---  # Mathlib and Lean documentation  Before applying a convention, read the workspace guide at:  `$PALIMPSEST_DOCUMENTATION/mathlib-reviewing.md`  That guide records the role hierarchy, review checklist, documentation ownership, upstream links, and a verification date. Follow its linked upstream source when the local summary and upstream documentation disagree. For any nontrivial convention finding, open the relevant upstream page or current mathlib source when network/reference access is available, and record the source if it materially informs the durable review.  For mathlib-style review, check at least:  - contribution scope and PR lifecycle; - naming and source style; - module documentation and declaration docstrings; - file placement and import hierarchy; - existing declarations and appropriate generality; - API integration, attributes, instances, and canonical forms; - deterministic validation against the exact reviewed commit.  For a non-obvious review point, the Palimpsest reference tools can search public mathlib pull requests and read their discussions. They may also search public Lean Zulip when the workspace has optional credentials. Search proportionally: current source and documentation are primary, precedent supports an analogy, and ordinary checks do not need a reference. Record only sources that materially informed the durable review.  Mathlib and Lean core have separate conventions. Do not apply one project's rules mechanically to another project. Upstream submission is human-owned and separate from work on the local Palimpsest forge. 
